<div style="width: 100%; background-color: #1a5276; color: white; text-align: center; padding: 15px 0; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Time for Exercise</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Like Walking in the Afternoon.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Comprehend the gist of short stories and news items. Interactive: Engage in simple discussions on lessons or current events.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can comprehend the gist of short stories about exercise (e.g., "The story is about a group of friends who play sports."). Can participate in simple discussions about different types of exercises (e.g., "What exercises do you like?" "I like to swim."). Can summarize the gist of news items related to health (e.g., "This news is about being healthy.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards with pictures of different exercises: "walking," "swimming," "running," "dancing," "cycling," "playing football." The teacher asks, "What do you see?" and encourages students to say the words in English if they know them. The teacher says, "Today we will listen about 'Time for Exercise!'"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary using flashcards: "walk," "exercise," "afternoon," "swimming," "running," "dancing," "healthy." The teacher highlights the /k/ sound in "can" (e.g., "I can walk.") and the /s/ sound in "swim" (e.g., "I like to swim."). The teacher segments multisyllabic words by clapping: "ex-er-cise," "cy-cling," "well-ness." The teacher says, "I like walking in the afternoon. It is healthy." and points to a picture.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of new vocabulary.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a picture of someone running: "Is this swimming or running?" (Expected answer: "Running.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a picture of someone dancing: "Is dancing fun?" (Expected answer: "Yes, it is fun!")</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Time for Exercise".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short audio story about two friends, Leo and Mia, discussing their favorite exercises. The audio will include target vocabulary (walk, run, swim, dance, sport, healthy, afternoon) and grammar (Present simple, "I like + -ing," "Do you like...?").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Receptive: Comprehend the gist of short stories about exercise.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First listening (Gist):</b> The teacher plays the audio once. Students listen and then point to the picture that best shows what the story is about (e.g., a group of friends doing sports vs. friends eating). Teacher asks, "What is the story about? Friends playing sports or friends eating?" (Expected answer: "Friends playing sports.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second listening (True/False):</b> The teacher plays the audio again. Students listen and decide if simple statements are true or false.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Statement 1: "Mia likes swimming." (True)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Statement 2: "Leo likes dancing." (False – he likes running)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Statement 3: "They exercise in the morning." (False – they exercise in the afternoon)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening for specific information about exercise preferences, using Present Simple questions ("Do you like...?"), affirmative sentences ("I like + -ing"), and "What type of exercise do you like?" with target vocabulary (walk, swim, run, dance, cycle, healthy).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Listening (Word Check):</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with pictures of exercises (swimming, running, dancing, walking, cycling) and the corresponding words underneath. Students listen to short audio clips of individual words (e.g., "swimming," "running," "walking") and circle the word they hear. Then, they listen to short sentences (e.g., "I like swimming.") and point to the correct picture.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Listening (Sentence Completion):</b> The teacher plays parts of sentences from the original audio or new similar sentences. Students listen and complete sentences on their worksheet using the target vocabulary. For example: "I like _____ in the afternoon." (listening for "walking"). "What type of exercise do you like? I like _____." (listening for "swimming").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Listen & Check):</b> Students work in pairs. Each student has a set of exercise picture cards. Student A says, "I like swimming." or "Do you like running?" Student B listens and points to the correct picture, or answers "Yes, I do." or "No, I don't." Then they switch roles. The teacher encourages them to use the pronunciation for /k/ and /s/ clearly.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Time for Exercise".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Listen and Guess the Activity" in small groups (3-4 students).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students sit in small groups. Each group receives a set of picture cards showing various exercises (walk, run, swim, dance, cycle, play football).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One student (the "speaker") secretly picks an exercise card. Without showing the card, the speaker describes the activity using simple sentences (e.g., "You can do this in the water. It is healthy. I like ____ing." or "You use your legs a lot. It makes you active. I do this every day."). They should avoid saying the name of the exercise directly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The other students (the "listeners") in the group listen carefully to the description. They discuss in English, asking questions like, "Is it swimming?" or "Do you like running?" (Pragmatic: Asking about preferences).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The first student to correctly guess the activity by naming it (e.g., "It's swimming!") becomes the next speaker.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, encouraging students with phrases like, "You can do it!" (Sociolinguistic: Encouraging others) and prompting them to use target vocabulary and grammar.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on listening comprehension and minimal spoken output.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> "Listen and Match." The teacher plays a short audio clip (e.g., "This news is about being healthy. Many people like walking in the afternoon. It is fun!"). Students listen and then match the audio gist to one of three pictures on a worksheet (e.g., Picture A: people walking, Picture B: people eating junk food, Picture C: people sleeping). They also answer a simple "What do they like to do?" question by circling the correct image (walking, running, swimming).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can comprehend the gist of the short audio.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can correctly match audio content to visuals.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Shows understanding of target vocabulary through non-verbal responses (pointing, circling).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Engages collaboratively in group activities (listening to peers).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can understand some words about exercise. (Circle: Yes / A little / No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can understand short sentences about what people like to do. (Circle: Yes / A little / No)</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What new exercise words did I hear today?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was easy or difficult about listening to the story/descriptions?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this listening-focused lesson? (e.g., flashcards, audio story, pair work for immediate feedback).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students to better comprehend spoken information, especially regarding distinguishing between similar-sounding words or understanding longer sentences? (e.g., more phonemic awareness activities, pre-teaching phrases).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Next Lesson:</b> In Lesson 2, students will use the same vocabulary and concepts about "Time for Exercise" to develop reading comprehension skills, focusing on reading short texts about different sports and healthy activities.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">2-3 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>